          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "18"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.184,
            0.069,
            0.488,
            0.086
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part I"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.108,
            0.886,
            0.165
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Note that the algebraic expressions in Example 1 and Example 2 involve two variables, whereas the algebraic expression in Example 3 involves only one variable."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.166,
            0.886,
            0.436
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Expressions such as 4x, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x^{2}+1,2y-5,5y^{3}+y^{2}+2y-1,3z+7"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " are algebraic expressions that involve only one variable: x, y or z. In this chapter, we will restrict our discussion to algebraic expressions involving only one variable. You may have noticed that in an algebraic expression, the powers of a variable also appear. For example, in the expression "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "X^{2} + 5X + 1"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , the highest power of x is 2, whereas in the expression "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "5 y ^ { 3 }   +   y ^ { 2 }   -   8 ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , the highest power of the variable y is 3. Further, in the expression "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "5 y ^ { 3 } + y ^ { 2 } + 2 y - 1"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , the coefficient of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "y ^ { 3 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is 5, that of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "y ^ { 2 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is 1, that of y is 2 and the constant term is –1. Such algebraic expressions involving one variable and its powers are called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "one-variable polynomials",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "univariate polynomials",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", or when the context is clear, simply "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "polynomials",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". (‘univariate’ means ‘having one variable’). The highest power of the variable in a polynomial is called its "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "degree",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". For example:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.125,
            0.436,
            0.885,
            0.476
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "5 y ^ { 3 } + y ^ { 2 } + 2 y - 1"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is a polynomial of degree 3. Such polynomials are called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "cubic polynomials",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.119,
            0.476,
            0.885,
            0.517
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(ii) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x ^ { 2 } + 5 x + 1"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is a polynomial of degree 2. Such polynomials are called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "quadratic polynomials",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.517,
            0.885,
            0.558
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(iii) 3z + 7 is a polynomial of degree 1. Such polynomials are called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "linear polynomials",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.559,
            0.886,
            0.62
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(iv) The constant 8 is a polynomial of degree 0 as it can be written as "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "8 x ^ { 0 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " in which the power of the variable x is 0. Such polynomials are called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "constant polynomials",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.374,
            0.643,
            0.621,
            0.67
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Exercise Set 2.1"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.685,
            0.678,
            0.707
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. Find the degrees of the following polynomials:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.185,
            0.706,
            0.359,
            0.725
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "2x^{2}-5x+3"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.178,
            0.728,
            0.341,
            0.749
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(ii) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "y^{3} + 2y - 1"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.172,
            0.75,
            0.268,
            0.77
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(iii) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "^ { - 9 }"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.173,
            0.772,
            0.297,
            0.792
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(iv) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "4 \\widetilde { z - 3 }"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.799,
            0.611,
            0.821
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Write polynomials of degrees 1, 2 and 3."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.824,
            0.77,
            0.865
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. What are the coefficients of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "X ^ { 2 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "X ^ { 3 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " in the polynomial "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "X^{4}-3X^{3}+6X^{2}-2X+7?"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
